{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_tcio4h2b4oobweji2r76o7erurj5cy4ek4d6yvlzpajq2z2cpjoa","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Both artifacts written.\n\n## Files\n\n- `/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-RqaJY6/translation/phaseA/errors_5.md` — **29 rows** (5.1–5.29)\n- `/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-RqaJY6/translation/phaseA/configs_5.md` — **89 rows** (5.1–5.89)\n\n## Key findings\n\n**Zero `return -1` and zero runtime `assert()` in all of area 5.** Every `int`-returning function returns `0` on every path that returns. The only failure mechanism is `sodium_misuse()` (misuse handler, then `abort()`), and all **nine** call sites live in `chacha20/stream_chacha20.c`. salsa20, salsa2012, salsa208, xsalsa20 and xchacha20 perform **no** length or counter validation whatsoever. The three `COMPILER_ASSERT`s (two in `stream_xchacha20.c`, one each in `stream_ref`/`stream_ietf_ext_ref`) are compile-time-only (`sizeof(char[(X)?1:-1])`).\n\n**Six of the nine misuse checks are dead branches on this build.** `crypto_stream_chacha20_MESSAGEBYTES_MAX == SODIUM_SIZE_MAX == min(UINT64_MAX, SIZE_MAX) == UINT64_MAX` on LP64, and the length parameters are `unsigned long long`, so `mlen > UINT64_MAX` is never true. Only the two `ietf_MESSAGEBYTES_MAX` (= `64*2^32` = `274877906944`) checks in `crypto_stream_chacha20_ietf` / `_ietf_xor` and the 32-bit counter guard in `_ietf_xor_ic` are reachable.\n\n**The most important finding — a guard-underflow hole in `crypto_stream_chacha20_ietf_xor_ic`** (`stream_chacha20.c:145-148`):\n\n```c\nif ((unsigned long long) ic >\n    (64ULL * (1ULL << 32)) / 64ULL - (mlen + 63ULL) / 64ULL) {\n```\n\nThe RHS is `4294967296 - ceil(mlen/64)` in unsigned 64-bit arithmetic. For `mlen > 2^38` it **underflows** to ~`2^64`, so `ic` (a `uint32_t`, max `0xFFFFFFFF`) can never exceed it and the guard silently passes. It then calls `_ietf_ext_xor_ic`, whose own check is dead. Result: `_ietf_xor_ic` is the one IETF entry point that **never** enforces `ietf_MESSAGEBYTES_MAX`, and oversized messages get **silent** 32-bit counter overflow carrying into nonce word 0 (keystream reuse) — no return code, no misuse.\n\n**Exact reachable boundaries for the `_ietf_xor_ic` guard:** `mlen ∈ [1,64]` → limit `0xFFFFFFFF`, never fires; `mlen ∈ [65,128]` → `ic == 0xFFFFFFFF` fires; `mlen == 2^38` → limit `0`, any `ic >= 1` fires; `mlen == 0` → limit `2^32`, never fires.\n\n**Counter-overflow behaviour differs by nonce layout, and is always silent.** In `chacha20_ref.c:182-187` the carry goes `j12 → j13`. For the *original* 8-byte-nonce layout `j13` is the counter high word (correct 64-bit increment, wrapping silently at `2^64`); for the *ietf* layout (`chacha_ietf_ivsetup`) `j13` is nonce word 0, so the counter overflows **into the IV** — the documented `_ext` behaviour. `crypto_stream_chacha20_ietf_ext_xor_ic` is the only way to reach this with a small message, since `_ietf_xor_ic` would reject it. Salsa20's `in[8..15]` 64-bit counter likewise wraps silently (final carry out of `in[15]` is dropped). `crypto_stream_xchacha20_xor_ic` takes a `uint64_t ic` and routes to the *original* chacha20 path, so no IETF constraint applies to it at all.\n\nAccessors and `*_keygen` cannot fail (constant returns; `randombytes_buf` aborts internally). Both `_pick_best_implementation` functions unconditionally select the `*_ref_implementation` and return `0`, so there is no dispatch axis to sweep."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11169,"cache_read_input_tokens":61645,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":11169,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01WVtpnaWpn7YaaTBTwmJRVd","session_id":"b566403e-4c11-4a9b-ad3a-d43b1f08afd2","uuid":"2960ec4f-0714-45df-8838-f4e2fddb385c","times