{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_q6bihnq73lwd24wzugiyo5nfkv75ibaljecr2fnr42jtfx2jskxq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Done. Both Phase-A artifacts for area 3 are written.\n\n**Files**\n- `/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-RqaJY6/translation/phaseA/errors_3.md` — **110 rows** (3.1–3.110)\n- `/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-RqaJY6/translation/phaseA/configs_3.md` — **131 rows** (3.1–3.131)\n\nNothing outside `translation/phaseA/` was touched; `c_src/` was read-only.\n\n**Key findings that matter for the port**\n\n1. **No `HAVE_*` macros confirmed relevant in 3 places**: `hash_sha256_cp.c` uses the scalar `#else` transform (the `HAVE_SHA256_ARMCRYPTO` NEON path is dead), `blake2b_increment_counter` uses the non-`HAVE_TI_MODE` 64-bit-pair carry, and `blake2b_pick_best_implementation` unconditionally lands on `blake2b_compress_ref`.\n\n2. **SHA-2 has literally zero error surface.** Every `crypto_hash_sha256/512_*` function returns `0` unconditionally; the only conditional is `if (inlen <= 0U) return 0;` which must be a *true* no-op (`count` is not advanced). Both `final`s `sodium_memzero` the whole state, so double-`final` and `update`-after-`final` silently produce a digest computed from an all-zero IV — no error is reported. That silent-reuse behavior is observable and has to be decided on explicitly in the port.\n\n3. **SHA-3 / XOF error signalling is unusual**: `sha3_update`/`*_ref_update` return `-1` on absorb-after-finalize **but still mutate state and absorb the data** (extra permute, phase reset). `sha3_final` returns `-1` on the second call yet still writes `outlen` bytes. `*_ref_squeeze` has no error path at all — squeeze-after-squeeze is legal stream continuation.\n\n4. **TurboSHAKE domain byte is completely unvalidated.** I looked for range checks across all four XOFs and the headers: none exist. `init_with_domain` stores any `0x00..0xFF` verbatim, so `0x00` and `0x80` are accepted despite the spec restricting the separation byte to `0x01..0x7F`. The port must not add validation. The `offset == RATE-1` fused-pad path (`domain ^ 0x80`) makes these edge values behaviorally distinct, so I gave it its own config row.\n\n5. **generichash/blake2b has three genuinely surprising behaviors**, all captured as rows:\n   - `BYTES_MIN` (16) and `KEYBYTES_MIN` (16) are **never enforced** — only `outlen == 0` and `outlen/keylen > 64` are rejected. `outlen`/`keylen` of 1..15 return `0`.\n   - **`key == NULL` with `keylen > 0` is asymmetric**: the one-shot `crypto_generichash_blake2b` reaches `blake2b()`'s check and **aborts** via `sodium_misuse()`, while `crypto_generichash_blake2b_init` silently routes it to the *unkeyed* `blake2b_init`.\n   - **`crypto_generichash_final` with `outlen` ≠ the init `outlen` is not an error** — `blake2b_final` validates only `1..64` and `memcpy`s from the 64-byte digest buffer, so it returns `0` and emits a prefix of the init-`outlen` digest.\n   - Also: `crypto_generichash_blake2b_final`'s `assert(outlen <= UINT8_MAX)` is *not* paired with a `return -1`, so under `NDEBUG` an `outlen` of 256 truncates to `0` and aborts inside `blake2b_final`. The only `-1` in `blake2b-ref.c` is the double-`final` `is_lastblock` check.\n   - `blake2b_state.last_node` is dead in this build (zeroed by `init0`, never set by any entry point), so `f[1]` always stays `0`.\n\n6. **shorthash is fully infallible** — no NULL/length checks anywhere; `k` is dereferenced as two `LOAD64_LE`. Note `b = ((uint64_t) inlen) << 56` means `inlen` aliases mod 256 in the length byte, which I flagged as a config row rather than an error."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":288,"cache_read_input_tokens":108502,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":288,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null}