{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_5mb4c3lmlymctpmi3kddsvxr6pki4qqxam5a7d3o2vffp6k5dnga","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_bdrk_01Kr9rUnRuaq1zmq1uPg2UXu","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-RqaJY6/translation/phaseA/configs_1.md","content":"## Area 1 — sodium core + randombytes\n\nConfiguration surface: valid-input combinations that the compiled C actually branches on.\nSame build assumptions as `errors_1.md` (x86-64 Linux, no `HAVE_*`, `-std=c99`, asserts live, `HAVE_LINUX_COMPATIBLE_GETRANDOM` and `BLOCK_ON_DEV_RANDOM` active, `page_size == DEFAULT_PAGE_SIZE == 0x10000`, `CANARY_SIZE == 16`, `GARBAGE_VALUE == 0xdb`, `TLS` empty).\n\n### version.h / runtime.h / core.h\n\n| # | entry point(s) | configuration (options set + input shape) | [ ] |\n|---|----------------|--------------------------------------------|-----|\n| 1.1 | `sodium_version_string` | any call | [ ] |\n| 1.2 | `sodium_library_version_major` / `sodium_library_version_minor` | any call → `SODIUM_LIBRARY_VERSION_MAJOR == 30`, `MINOR == 0` | [ ] |\n| 1.3 | `sodium_library_minimal` | `SODIUM_LIBRARY_MINIMAL` **undefined** in this build → the `#else` arm → `0` | [ ] |\n| 1.4 | `sodium_init` | very first call in the process (`initialized == 0`) → runs all `_pick_best_implementation` hooks + `randombytes_stir()` + `_sodium_alloc_init()` → `0` | [ ] |\n| 1.5 | `sodium_init` | second and subsequent calls (`initialized != 0`) → early-out → `1` | [ ] |\n| 1.6 | `sodium_init` | called *after* a lazily-initialising randombytes call (e.g. `randombytes_buf` first) — `randombytes_stir()` runs a second time on an already-stirred implementation | [ ] |\n| 1.7 | `sodium_crit_enter` / `sodium_crit_leave` | no-op `#else` implementations: enter-then-leave, leave without enter, nested enter, leave twice — all `0`, no `locked` bookkeeping | [ ] |\n| 1.8 | `sodium_set_misuse_handler` | `handler == NULL` (clears the handler) → a later `sodium_misuse()` goes straight to `abort()` | [ ] |\n| 1.9 | `sodium_set_misuse_handler` | `handler != NULL` → a later `sodium_misuse()` calls the handler first, then `abort()` (handler cannot prevent the abort by returning) | [ ] |\n| 1.10 | `sodium_set_misuse_handler` | called twice — second handler replaces the first | [ ] |\n| 1.11 | `_sodium_runtime_get_cpu_features` | first call vs. repeated calls; `_cpu_features.initialized` set to 1 either way; return value always `-1` | [ ] |\n| 1.12 | `sodium_runtime_has_*` (all 12) | queried before `sodium_init()` (static zero state) vs. after `sodium_init()` (still all zero, since both probes bail out early) — must be identical | [ ] |\n\n### utils.c — memzero / stackzero\n\n| # | entry point(s) | configuration (options set + input shape) | [ ] |\n|---|----------------|--------------------------------------------|-----|\n| 1.13 | `sodium_memzero` | `len == 0` → the volatile `while (i < len)` loop body never executes; `pnt` is never dereferenced (a dangling/NULL `pnt` with `len == 0` is safe) | [ ] |\n| 1.14 | `sodium_memzero` | `len == 1` | [ ] |\n| 1.15 | `sodium_memzero` | `len` not a multiple of the word size (e.g. 7, 17, 31) — the compiled path is a byte-at-a-time volatile loop, so no alignment special-casing | [ ] |\n| 1.16 | `sodium_memzero` | `len` large (e.g. 4096) and/or a sub-slice of a larger buffer — bytes outside `[pnt, pnt+len)` must be untouched | [ ] |\n| 1.17 | `sodium_stackzero` | any `len` including 0 — compiled body is **empty** (no `HAVE_C_VARARRAYS`, no `HAVE_ALLOCA`); observable behaviour is \"does nothing\" | [ ] |\n\n### utils.c — constant-time compare / is_zero\n\n| # | entry point(s) | configuration (options set + input shape) | [ ] |\n|---|----------------|--------------------------------------------|-----|\n| 1.18 | `sodium_memcmp` | `len == 0` → `d` stays 0 → `0` (equal), pointers never dereferenced | [ ] |\n| 1.19 | `sodium_memcmp` | `len == 1`, equal | [ ] |\n| 1.20 | `sodium_memcmp` | `b1_ == b2_` (same pointer), `len > 0` → `0` | [ ] |\n| 1.21 | `sodium_memcmp` | equal buffers at the crypto-relevant sizes 16 / 32 / 64 | [ ] |\n| 1.22 | `sodium_memcmp` | differ only in byte 0 / only in the last byte / in every byte → `-1` in all cases | [ ] |\n| 1.23 | `sodium_memcmp` | `b1` all-`0x00` vs `b2` all-`0xff` → `-1` (exercises the `(d - 1) >> 8` sentinel with `d == 0xff`) | [ ] |\n| 1.24 | `sodium_compare` | `len == 0` → `gt == 0`, `eq == 1` → `0` | [ ] |\n| 1.25 | `sodium_compare` | equal buffers, `len` 1 / 8 / 32 → `0` | [ ] |\n| 1.26 | `sodium_compare` | differ only in the **highest** index (most significant, little-endian) — `b1 < b2` → `-1`; swapped → `1` | [ ] |\n| 1.27 | `sodium_compare` | differ only in index 0 (least significant) — proves little-endian ordering, not `memcmp` ordering | [ ] |\n| 1.28 | `sodium_compare` | `b1` all-`0x00` vs `b2` all-`0xff` → `-1`; and `0xff…` vs `0x00…` → `1` | [ ] |\n| 1.29 | `sodium_compare` | `len` 8 / 12 / 24 / 32 / 64 — the `HAVE_AMD64_ASM` fast paths are **not** compiled, so all lengths take the identical portable loop; verify no length is special | [ ] |\n| 1.30 | `sodium_is_zero` | `nlen == 0` → `1` (vacuously zero) | [ ] |\n| 1.31 | `sodium_is_zero` | all-zero buffer, `nlen` 1 / 16 / 32 → `1` | [ ] |\n| 1.32 | `sodium_is_zero` | single non-zero byte at index 0 / at the last index / `0x01` only → `0` | [ ] |\n| 1.33 | `sodium_is_zero` | all-`0xff` → `0` | [ ] |\n\n### utils.c — increment / add / sub (portable loop only; the `HAVE_AMD64_ASM` 8/12/24/64-byte paths are NOT compiled)\n\n| # | entry point(s) | configuration (options set + input shape) | [ ] |\n|---|----------------|--------------------------------------------|-----|\n| 1.34 | `sodium_increment` | `nlen == 0` → no-op, `n` never dereferenced | [ ] |\n| 1.35 | `sodium_increment` | `nlen == 1`, `n == {0x00}` → `{0x01}` | [ ] |\n| 1.36 | `sodium_increment` | `nlen == 1`, `n == {0xff}` → `{0x00}` (wrap, carry dropped) | [ ] |\n| 1.37 | `sodium_increment` | carry across a single byte boundary: `{0xff, 0x00}` → `{0x00, 0x01}` | [ ] |\n| 1.38 | `sodium_increment` | `nlen == 8` / `12` / `24` (the ex-asm sizes) with `n` all-`0xff` → all-`0x00`; and `n` all-`0x00` → `{0x01, 0, …}` | [ ] |\n| 1.39 | `sodium_increment` | `nlen == 32` (nonce/counter size), full carry chain from all-`0xff` | [ ] |\n| 1.40 | `sodium_add` | `len == 0` → no-op | [ ] |\n| 1.41 | `sodium_add` | `len == 1`, no carry (`0x01 + 0x01`) and with wrap (`0xff + 0x01` → `0x00`) | [ ] |\n| 1.42 | `sodium_add` | `len == 8` / `12` / `24` / `32` / `64` (ex-asm sizes) — `a` all-`0xff` plus `b == {0x01, 0, …}` → all-zero | [ ] |\n| 1.43 | `sodium_add` | full carry chain: `a` all-`0xff`, `b` all-`0xff` | [ ] |\n| 1.44 | `sodium_add` | `a == b` (aliasing the same buffer) — doubling | [ ] |\n| 1.45 | `sodium_sub` | `len == 0` → no-op | [ ] |\n| 1.46 | `sodium_sub` | `len == 1`, no borrow (`0x02 - 0x01`) and with borrow (`0x00 - 0x01` → `0xff`) | [ ] |\n| 1.47 | `sodium_sub` | `len == 64` (the ex-asm size) — `a` all-zero minus `b == {0x01, 0, …}` → all-`0xff` (borrow propagates the whole length) | [ ] |\n| 1.48 | `sodium_sub` | `a == b` bytewise → all-zero result; and `a` and `b` the **same pointer** | [ ] |\n| 1.49 | `sodium_sub` | `len` 8 / 24 / 32 (not the asm size) — must be identical to `len == 64` semantics | [ ] |\n\n### codecs.c — hex\n\n| # | entry point(s) | configuration (options set + input shape) | [ ] |\n|---|----------------|--------------------------------------------|-----|\n| 1.50 | `sodium_bin2hex` | `bin_len == 0`, `hex_maxlen == 1` → writes only the terminating NUL, returns `hex` | [ ] |\n| 1.51 | `sodium_bin2hex` | `hex_maxlen == 2 * bin_len + 1` exactly (minimum accepted size) | [ ] |\n| 1.52 | `sodium_bin2hex` | `hex_maxlen > 2 * bin_len + 1` — only `2*bin_len + 1` bytes are written, the tail of `hex` must be untouched | [ ] |\n| 1.53 | `sodium_bin2hex` | bin bytes covering both nibble branches: `0x00`, `0x09`, `0x0a`, `0x0f`, `0x10`, `0xa0`, `0xff` → lowercase `0-9a-f` output, low nibble first in the pair | [ ] |\n| 1.54 | `sodium_hex2bin` | `ignore == NULL`, `bin_len != NULL`, `hex_end != NULL`, even-length all-valid lowercase hex, `bin_maxlen == hex_len / 2` exactly | [ ] |\n| 1.55 | `sodium_hex2bin` | `bin_len == NULL` **and** `hex_end == NULL` (both out-params omitted) with fully-consumed valid input → `0` | [ ] |\n| 1.56 | `sodium_hex2bin` | `bin_len != NULL`, `hex_end == NULL` — strict mode: all of `hex` must be consumed | [ ] |\n| 1.57 | `sodium_hex2bin` | `bin_len == NULL`, `hex_end != NULL` — the end pointer is set but no length is reported | [ ] |\n| 1.58 | `sodium_hex2bin` | `hex_len == 0` → `0`, `*bin_len == 0`, `*hex_end == hex` | [ ] |\n| 1.59 | `sodium_hex2bin` | uppercase hex (`\"AB\"`), lowercase (`\"ab\"`), and mixed (`\"aB\"`) — the `c & ~32U` branch | [ ] |\n| 1.60 | `sodium_hex2bin` | `bin_maxlen == 0` with `hex_len == 0` → `0`; `bin` never written | [ ] |\n| 1.61 | `sodium_hex2bin` | `ignore = \":\"` with separators **between** byte pairs (`\"aa:bb:cc\"`) — skipped only while `state == 0` | [ ] |\n| 1.62 | `sodium_hex2bin` | `ignore = \":\"` with a separator **inside** a byte pair (`\"a:abb\"`) — `state != 0` so the ignore branch is *not* taken; the char terminates the scan | [ ] |\n| 1.63 | `sodium_hex2bin` | `ignore = \" \\n\"` with leading ignorable chars (`\"  aabb\"`) — skipped at the very start | [ ] |\n| 1.64 | `sodium_hex2bin` | `ignore` non-NULL with **trailing** ignorable chars (`\"aabb  \"`) and `hex_end != NULL` → `0` with `*hex_end` pointing **at** the trailing chars (unlike `sodium_base642bin`, `hex2bin` does *not* skip trailing ignore chars) | [ ] |\n| 1.65 | `sodium_hex2bin` | `ignore` containing a character that is itself a hex digit (e.g. `ignore = \"a\"`) — the hex-digit branch wins, the ignore set is only consulted for non-hex chars | [ ] |\n| 1.66 | `sodium_hex2bin` | `ignore = \"\"` (empty, non-NULL) — behaves like `ignore == NULL` because `strchr(\"\", c)` is NULL for any non-NUL `c` | [ ] |\n| 1.67 | `sodium_hex2bin` | valid hex followed by a non-hex char with `hex_end != NULL` (`\"aabbZZ\"`) → `0`, `*bin_len == 2`, `*hex_end` at the `'Z'` | [ ] |\n| 1.68 | `sodium_hex2bin` | embedded NUL inside `hex` with `hex_len` spanning it — the NUL is not a hex digit, so it terminates the scan (and `strchr(ignore, 0)` would match the ignore string's own terminator when `ignore != NULL` — worth pinning) | [ ] |\n\n### codecs.c — base64 (4 variants × input shape)\n\n`sodium_base64_VARIANT_ORIGINAL = 1`, `ORIGINAL_NO_PADDING = 3`, `URLSAFE = 5`, `URLSAFE_NO_PADDING = 7`.\n`VARIANT_NO_PADDING_MASK = 0x2`, `VARIANT_URLSAFE_MASK = 0x4`. Valid variants are exactly `{1,3,5,7}`.\n\n| # | entry point(s) | configuration (options set + input shape) | [ ] |\n|---|----------------|--------------------------------------------|-----|\n| 1.69 | `sodium_base64_encoded_len` / `sodium_base64_ENCODED_LEN` (macro) | VARIANT_ORIGINAL, `bin_len == 0` → `1`; and `bin_len % 3 == 0, > 0` → `4*(n/3) + 1` | [ ] |\n| 1.70 | `sodium_base64_encoded_len` / macro | VARIANT_ORIGINAL, `bin_len % 3 == 1` → `4*(n/3) + 4 + 1` | [ ] |\n| 1.71 | `sodium_base64_encoded_len` / macro | VARIANT_ORIGINAL, `bin_len % 3 == 2` → `4*(n/3) + 4 + 1` | [ ] |\n| 1.72 | `sodium_base64_encoded_len` / macro | VARIANT_ORIGINAL_NO_PADDING, `bin_len % 3 == 0 / 1 / 2` → `+0 / +2 / +3` before the `+1` | [ ] |\n| 1.73 | `sodium_base64_encoded_len` / macro | VARIANT_URLSAFE — identical lengths to ORIGINAL (URLSAFE bit does not affect length) | [ ] |\n| 1.74 | `sodium_base64_encoded_len` / macro | VARIANT_URLSAFE_NO_PADDING — identical lengths to ORIGINAL_NO_PADDING | [ ] |\n| 1.75 | `sodium_base64_encoded_len` vs. the `sodium_base64_ENCODED_LEN` macro | same `(bin_len, variant)` pairs must agree; the macro additionally clamps to `SIZE_MAX` where the function aborts | [ ] |\n| 1.76 | `sodium_bin2base64` | VARIANT_ORIGINAL, `bin_len == 0`, `b64_maxlen == 1` → empty string | [ ] |\n| 1.77 | `sodium_bin2base64` | VARIANT_ORIGINAL, `bin_len % 3 == 0` (3, 6, 300) — no padding needed even in a padded variant | [ ] |\n| 1.78 | `sodium_bin2base64` | VARIANT_ORIGINAL, `bin_len % 3 == 1` → 2 trailing `'='` | [ ] |\n| 1.79 | `sodium_bin2base64` | VARIANT_ORIGINAL, `bin_len % 3 == 2` → 1 trailing `'='` | [ ] |\n| 1.80 | `sodium_bin2base64` | VARIANT_ORIGINAL_NO_PADDING, `bin_len % 3 == 0` (identical output to 1.77) | [ ] |\n| 1.81 | `sodium_bin2base64` | VARIANT_ORIGINAL_NO_PADDING, `bin_len % 3 == 1` → `b64_len += 2 + (1 >> 1) == 2` chars, no `'='` | [ ] |\n| 1.82 | `sodium_bin2base64` | VARIANT_ORIGINAL_NO_PADDING, `bin_len % 3 == 2` → `b64_len += 2 + (2 >> 1) == 3` chars, no `'='` | [ ] |\n| 1.83 | `sodium_bin2base64` | VARIANT_URLSAFE, `bin_len % 3 == 0 / 1 / 2` — `b64_byte_to_urlsafe_char`, `'-'`/`'_'` for 62/63, padded | [ ] |\n| 1.84 | `sodium_bin2base64` | VARIANT_URLSAFE_NO_PADDING, `bin_len % 3 == 0 / 1 / 2` | [ ] |\n| 1.85 | `sodium_bin2base64` | bin bytes chosen so 6-bit indices `62` and `63` occur (e.g. `{0xfb, 0xef, 0xbe}`) — verifies `'+'`/`'/'` for ORIGINAL vs `'-'`/`'_'` for URLSAFE | [ ] |\n| 1.86 | `sodium_bin2base64` | bin bytes covering index ranges `<26` (`A-Z`), `26..51` (`a-z`), `52..61` (`0-9`) — the three `LT`/`GE` branches of `b64_byte_to_char` | [ ] |\n| 1.87 | `sodium_bin2base64` | `b64_maxlen == b64_len + 1` exactly (minimum accepted) → single NUL written | [ ] |\n| 1.88 | `sodium_bin2base64` | `b64_maxlen > b64_len + 1` — the `do { b64[b64_pos++] = 0; } while (b64_pos < b64_maxlen)` loop **zero-fills the entire remainder of `b64_maxlen`**, not just one NUL byte | [ ] |\n| 1.89 | `sodium_base642bin` | VARIANT_ORIGINAL, `b64_len % 4 == 0` with no `'='` (whole-block input), `ignore == NULL`, `bin_len != NULL`, `b64_end != NULL` | [ ] |\n| 1.90 | `sodium_base642bin` | VARIANT_ORIGINAL, input ending in one `'='` (`acc_len == 2` → 1 pad char consumed by `_sodium_base642bin_skip_padding`) | [ ] |\n| 1.91 | `sodium_base642bin` | VARIANT_ORIGINAL, input ending in two `'=='` (`acc_len == 4` → 2 pad chars consumed) | [ ] |\n| 1.92 | `sodium_base642bin` | VARIANT_ORIGINAL, `b64_len == 0` → `0`, `*bin_len == 0`, `*b64_end == b64` | [ ] |\n| 1.93 | `sodium_base642bin` | VARIANT_ORIGINAL_NO_PADDING with 2 trailing chars (`acc_len == 4`) — `skip_padding` is skipped entirely thanks to `VARIANT_NO_PADDING_MASK` | [ ] |\n| 1.94 | `sodium_base642bin` | VARIANT_ORIGINAL_NO_PADDING with 3 trailing chars (`acc_len == 2`) | [ ] |\n| 1.95 | `sodium_base642bin` | VARIANT_ORIGINAL_NO_PADDING with `b64_len % 4 == 0` (identical to the padded case minus padding) | [ ] |\n| 1.96 | `sodium_base642bin` | VARIANT_ORIGINAL_NO_PADDING fed input that *does* contain `'='`: `'='` is not a valid b64 char → loop breaks; with `b64_end != NULL` → `0` and `*b64_end` at the `'='`; with `b64_end == NULL` → `-1`/`EINVAL` | [ ] |\n| 1.97 | `sodium_base642bin` | VARIANT_URLSAFE with `'-'` and `'_'` in the input (`b64_urlsafe_char_to_byte`), padded, all three `acc_len` residues | [ ] |\n| 1.98 | `sodium_base642bin` | VARIANT_URLSAFE_NO_PADDING with `'-'`/`'_'`, all three residues | [ ] |\n| 1.99 | `sodium_base642bin` | cross-alphabet rejection as a *valid* stop: URLSAFE variant given `'+'`/`'/'`, and ORIGINAL variant given `'-'`/`'_'` — with `b64_end != NULL` the decode stops cleanly at that char and returns `0` | [ ] |\n| 1.100 | `sodium_base642bin` | `'A'` in the input — `b64_char_to_byte` returns `0` for `'A'` and relies on `(EQ(x,0) & (EQ(c,'A') ^ 0xFF))` to distinguish it from an invalid char; input `\"AAAA\"` must decode to three `0x00` bytes | [ ] |\n| 1.101 | `sodium_base642bin` | `ignore = \" \\n\\r\"` with ignorable chars interleaved between data chars (PEM-style wrapped input) | [ ] |\n| 1.102 | `sodium_base642bin` | `ignore` non-NULL with ignorable chars **inside** the padding run (e.g. `\"QQ=\\n=\"`) — `_sodium_base642bin_skip_padding` consults `ignore` too | [ ] |\n| 1.103 | `sodium_base642bin` | `ignore` non-NULL with **trailing** ignorable chars after the data/padding and `ret == 0` — the final `while (b64_pos < b64_len && strchr(ignore, …))` loop consumes them, so `*b64_end == b64 + b64_len` and `b64_end == NULL` also succeeds | [ ] |\n| 1.104 | `sodium_base642bin` | `ignore = \"\"` (empty, non-NULL) — the `ignore != NULL` branches are entered but `strchr` never matches; trailing-skip loop terminates immediately | [ ] |\n| 1.105 | `sodium_base642bin` | `bin_len == NULL` and `b64_end == NULL` (both omitted, strict mode) with fully-consumed padded input | [ ] |\n| 1.106 | `sodium_base642bin` | `bin_len != NULL`, `b64_end == NULL` — strict length check on the input | [ ] |\n| 1.107 | `sodium_base642bin` | `bin_len == NULL`, `b64_end != NULL` | [ ] |\n| 1.108 | `sodium_base642bin` | `bin_maxlen` exactly the decoded size (minimum accepted) vs. `bin_maxlen` larger (tail of `bin` must be untouched) | [ ] |\n| 1.109 | `sodium_bin2base64` → `sodium_base642bin` | round-trip for each of the 4 variants × each `bin_len % 3` residue × `bin_len == 0` | [ ] |\n| 1.110 | `sodium_bin2hex` → `sodium_hex2bin` | round-trip for `bin_len` 0 / 1 / 32 | [ ] |\n\n### codecs.c — ip2bin / bin2ip\n\n| # | entry point(s) | configuration (options set + input shape) | [ ] |\n|---|----------------|--------------------------------------------|-----|\n| 1.111 | `sodium_ip2bin` | IPv4 dotted quad `\"1.2.3.4\"` → IPv4-mapped form: `bin[0..9] == 0`, `bin[10] == bin[11] == 0xff`, `bin[12..15] == {1,2,3,4}` | [ ] |\n| 1.112 | `sodium_ip2bin` | IPv4 boundary values `\"0.0.0.0\"` and `\"255.255.255.255\"` | [ ] |\n| 1.113 | `sodium_ip2bin` | IPv4 with 1-, 2-, and 3-digit octets and leading zeros (`\"01.002.3.40\"`) — accepted since `digits <= 3` and `val <= 255` | [ ] |\n| 1.114 | `sodium_ip2bin` | `ip_len_` **larger** than `strlen(ip)` — the scan loop stops at the first NUL, so the excess is ignored | [ ] |\n| 1.115 | `sodium_ip2bin` | `ip_len_` **smaller** than `strlen(ip)` — the address is truncated at `ip_len_` (e.g. `\"1.2.3.45\"` with `ip_len_ == 7` → `1.2.3.4`); non-NUL-terminated input is legal | [ ] |\n| 1.116 | `sodium_ip2bin` | IPv6 with all 8 explicit groups `\"1:2:3:4:5:6:7:8\"` (no `colonp`, `tp == endp` exactly) | [ ] |\n| 1.117 | `sodium_ip2bin` | IPv6 with `\"::\"` in the middle `\"1:2::7:8\"` — `colonp` set, `memmove`/`memset` zero-fill path | [ ] |\n| 1.118 | `sodium_ip2bin` | IPv6 with a leading `\"::\"` (`\"::1\"`) — the `*p == ':'` prologue branch | [ ] |\n| 1.119 | `sodium_ip2bin` | IPv6 with a trailing `\"::\"` (`\"1::\"`) — `saw_xdigit == 0` at loop exit, `colonp` set | [ ] |\n| 1.120 | `sodium_ip2bin` | IPv6 `\"::\"` alone → all-zero `bin` (unspecified address) | [ ] |\n| 1.121 | `sodium_ip2bin` | IPv6 with embedded IPv4 after `\"::ffff:\"` (`\"::ffff:1.2.3.4\"`) — the `ch == '.'` branch calling `parse_ipv4(curtok, end, tp)`; must equal the result of `sodium_ip2bin(\"1.2.3.4\")` | [ ] |\n| 1.122 | `sodium_ip2bin` | IPv6 with embedded IPv4 and a non-`ffff` prefix (`\"64:ff9b::1.2.3.4\"`) | [ ] |\n| 1.123 | `sodium_ip2bin` | IPv6 with 8 explicit groups where the last is an embedded IPv4 (`\"1:2:3:4:5:6:1.2.3.4\"`) — `tp + 4 == endp` exactly | [ ] |\n| 1.124 | `sodium_ip2bin` | IPv6 groups of 1, 2, 3, and 4 hex digits (`\"a:bc:def:0123::\"`), uppercase (`\"FE80::1\"`) and lowercase, mixed case | [ ] |\n| 1.125 | `sodium_ip2bin` | IPv6 with a zone id: `\"fe80::1%eth0\"`, `\"fe80::1%1\"`, `\"fe80::1%a-b_c.d\"` — zone chars `[0-9a-zA-Z._-]`; `end` is moved back to the `'%'` so the zone is parsed but discarded | [ ] |\n| 1.126 | `sodium_ip2bin` | `bin` fully overwritten on success and untouched on failure — verify the 16 output bytes for both the IPv4 (`memset` 10 + `0xff 0xff` + 4-byte copy) and IPv6 (`memcpy` of 16) paths | [ ] |\n| 1.127 | `sodium_bin2ip` | `bin` = IPv4-mapped prefix + `{1,2,3,4}` → `\"1.2.3.4\"`, with `ip_maxlen == len + 1` exactly and with `ip_maxlen` larger | [ ] |\n| 1.128 | `sodium_bin2ip` | `bin` = IPv4-m