{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01Y9eLAnwtAY1GJ5pUvUBxud","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t## Area 6 — crypto_aead / secretbox / secretstream\n2\t\n3\tScope: `c_src/libsodium/crypto_aead/{aegis128l,aegis256,aes256gcm,chacha20poly1305,xchacha20poly1305}`,\n4\t`c_src/libsodium/crypto_secretbox/**`, `c_src/libsodium/crypto_secretstream/xchacha20poly1305/**`\n5\tplus the matching public headers.\n6\t\n7\t### Named sweeps used below\n8\t\n9\t* **`MLEN`** = `{0, 1, 15, 16, 17, 31, 32, 33, 63, 64, 65, 127, 128, 129}` — the mandatory\n10\t  short-message shape sweep. Every row that says \"`mlen ∈ MLEN`\" is one sub-case per element.\n11\t* **`ADLEN`** = `{ad = NULL & adlen = 0, ad = non-NULL & adlen = 0, 1, 15, 16, 17, 31, 32, 33}`\n12\t  — 9 sub-cases. The two `adlen == 0` variants are distinguished deliberately: `ad == NULL` with\n13\t  `adlen == 0` reaches `crypto_onetimeauth_poly1305_update(&state, NULL, 0)` /\n14\t  `memcpy(src, NULL, 0)`, which C technically leaves undefined but libsodium relies on.\n15\t* **`BIG_AEGIS128L`** = `{224, 255, 256, 257, 511, 512, 513, 1024, 4096}` — aegis128l `RATE == 32`\n16\t  (`aegis128l_common.h:1`) and the absorb loop consumes `RATE*2 == 64` at a time\n17\t  (`aegis128l_soft.c:172-182`), so multiples/off-by-ones of 32 and 64 exercise\n18\t  `absorb2` / `absorb` / the `% RATE` tail and `declast`.\n19\t* **`BIG_AEGIS256`** = `{112, 127, 128, 129, 255, 256, 257, 1024, 4096}` — aegis256 `RATE == 16`\n20\t  (`aegis256_common.h:1`), absorb2 consumes `2*RATE == 32`.\n21\t* **`BIG_CHACHA`** = `{4096, 65536, 131071, 131072, 131073, 262144, 262145}` — crosses the\n22\t  64-byte ChaCha20 block and the `STREAM_POLY1305_CHUNK == 131072` re-entry boundary\n23\t  (`aead_chacha20poly1305.c:20,52-61`), which is where the `ic` counter arithmetic\n24\t  (`ic += cl / 64U`) can go wrong.\n25\t* **`BIG_SECRETBOX`** = `{32, 33, 63, 64, 65, 4096, 131072, 131073, 262145}` — crosses the\n26\t  `64 - ZEROBYTES == 32` first-block special case (`crypto_secretbox_easy.c:50-52`) and the\n27\t  `STREAM_POLY1305_CHUNK` boundary (`crypto_secretbox_easy.c:71-82`).\n28\t\n29\t### Build-configuration constants that fix some axes\n30\t\n31\t* No `HAVE_*` macros ⇒ aegis128l/aegis256 always run the portable `*_soft.c` implementation;\n32\t  the aes256gcm **stub** family links, `crypto_aead_aes256gcm_is_available()` returns `0`, and\n33\t  all nine other aes256gcm entry points return `-1` with `errno = ENOSYS`. The aes256gcm rows\n34\t  below therefore have no positive/round-trip configurations at all — they are all\n35\t  \"must return -1 regardless of shape\" rows (cross-referenced to `errors_6.md` 6.30–6.39).\n36\t* `NSECBYTES == 0` for all six AEADs ⇒ **`nsec` is always `NULL`** in every row, and the\n37\t  implementations do `(void) nsec;`.\n38\t* ABYTES: aegis128l 32, aegis256 32, aes256gcm 16, chacha20poly1305 16,\n39\t  chacha20poly1305_ietf 16, xchacha20poly1305_ietf 16, secretbox MAC 16,\n40\t  secretstream 17 (`1 + 16`).\n41\t* NPUBBYTES: aegis128l 16, aegis256 32, aes256gcm 12, chacha20poly1305 **8** (original),\n42\t  chacha20poly1305_ietf **12**, xchacha20poly1305_ietf **24**, secretbox nonce 24,\n43\t  secretstream header 24.\n44\t* KEYBYTES: aegis128l **16**; everything else 32.\n45\t\n46\t### CONFIGURATION-SURFACE table\n47\t\n48\t| # | entry point(s) | configuration (options set + input shape) | [ ] |\n49\t|---|----------------|--------------------------------------------|-----|\n50\t| **aegis128l** (KEY 16, NPUB 16, ABYTES 32, RATE 32, portable soft impl) | | | |\n51\t| 6.1 | `crypto_aead_aegis128l_keybytes` / `_nsecbytes` / `_npubbytes` / `_abytes` / `_messagebytes_max` | constant getters; assert `16 / 0 / 16 / 32 / MIN(SIZE_MAX-32, 2^61-1)` | [ ] |\n52\t| 6.2 | `crypto_aead_aegis128l_keygen` | fill a 16-byte buffer; two successive calls differ; buffer fully written | [ ] |\n53\t| 6.3 | `crypto_aead_aegis128l_encrypt` + `_decrypt` | round trip, `ad = NULL`, `adlen = 0`, `nsec = NULL`, `clen_p != NULL`, `mlen_p != NULL`; `mlen ∈ MLEN`; assert `clen == mlen + 32`, `*mlen_p == mlen`, recovered `m` equal | [ ] |\n54\t| 6.4 | `crypto_aead_aegis128l_encrypt` + `_decrypt` | as 6.3 but `clen_p = NULL` on encrypt and `mlen_p = NULL` on decrypt; `mlen ∈ MLEN` | [ ] |\n55\t| 6.5 | `crypto_aead_aegis128l_encrypt` + `_decrypt` | `adlen ∈ ADLEN` × `mlen ∈ {0, 1, 32, 33, 64}`; ad byte-for-byte identical on both sides | [ ] |\n56\t| 6.6 | `crypto_aead_aegis128l_encrypt` + `_decrypt` | `mlen ∈ BIG_AEGIS128L`, `adlen ∈ {0, 64, 65, 128}` — exercises `aegis128l_absorb2` (64-byte stride), `absorb` (32-byte stride), the `adlen % 32` tail, and `aegis128l_declast` | [ ] |\n57\t| 6.7 | `crypto_aead_aegis128l_encrypt_detached` + `_decrypt_detached` | `maclen_p != NULL` (assert `*maclen_p == 32`), separate 32-byte `mac` buffer; `mlen ∈ MLEN`, `adlen ∈ ADLEN`; detached output must equal `encrypt` output split at `mlen` | [ ] |\n58\t| 6.8 | `crypto_aead_aegis128l_encrypt_detached` | `maclen_p = NULL`; `mlen ∈ MLEN` — assert identical ciphertext/mac to 6.7 | [ ] |\n59\t| 6.9 | `crypto_aead_aegis128l_decrypt_detached` | `m = NULL` (verify-only); valid mac ⇒ `0`, tampered mac ⇒ `-1`; `mlen ∈ MLEN` — exercises the `else` branches at `aegis128l_soft.c:225-229, 234` | [ ] |\n60\t| 6.10 | `crypto_aead_aegis128l_decrypt` / `_decrypt_detached` | `nsec` (out-param) `NULL` vs pointing at a poisoned 1-byte buffer; assert byte unmodified and result unchanged | [ ] |\n61\t| 6.11 | `crypto_aead_aegis128l_encrypt` / `_decrypt` | in-place: `c == m` and `m == c` aliasing for `mlen ∈ {0, 1, 32, 33, 64, 1024}` | [ ] |\n62\t| 6.12 | `crypto_aead_aegis128l_encrypt` / `_decrypt` | key/nonce corner shapes: all-zero `k`, all-`0xff` `k`, all-zero `npub`, all-`0xff` `npub`; `mlen ∈ {0, 32, 33}` | [ ] |\n63\t| 6.13 | `crypto_aead_aegis128l_encrypt` | fixed KAT vectors (deterministic `k`, `npub`, `m`, `ad`) — the portable soft path must match the reference AEGIS-128L tag/ciphertext | [ ] |\n64\t| **aegis256** (KEY 32, NPUB 32, ABYTES 32, RATE 16, portable soft impl) | | | |\n65\t| 6.14 | `crypto_aead_aegis256_keybytes` / `_nsecbytes` / `_npubbytes` / `_abytes` / `_messagebytes_max` | assert `32 / 0 / 32 / 32 / MIN(SIZE_MAX-32, 2^61-1)` | [ ] |\n66\t| 6.15 | `crypto_aead_aegis256_keygen` | fill a 32-byte buffer; two calls differ | [ ] |\n67\t| 6.16 | `crypto_aead_aegis256_encrypt` + `_decrypt` | round trip, `ad = NULL`/`adlen = 0`, `nsec = NULL`, out-length pointers non-NULL; `mlen ∈ MLEN`; assert `clen == mlen + 32` | [ ] |\n68\t| 6.17 | `crypto_aead_aegis256_encrypt` + `_decrypt` | as 6.16 with `clen_p = NULL` / `mlen_p = NULL`; `mlen ∈ MLEN` | [ ] |\n69\t| 6.18 | `crypto_aead_aegis256_encrypt` + `_decrypt` | `adlen ∈ ADLEN` × `mlen ∈ {0, 1, 16, 17, 32}` | [ ] |\n70\t| 6.19 | `crypto_aead_aegis256_encrypt` + `_decrypt` | `mlen ∈ BIG_AEGIS256`, `adlen ∈ {0, 32, 33, 64}` — exercises `aegis256_absorb2` (32-byte stride), `absorb` (16-byte stride), `adlen % 16` tail, `aegis256_declast` | [ ] |\n71\t| 6.20 | `crypto_aead_aegis256_encrypt_detached` + `_decrypt_detached` | `maclen_p != NULL` (`*maclen_p == 32`); `mlen ∈ MLEN`, `adlen ∈ ADLEN`; must agree with the combined API | [ ] |\n72\t| 6.21 | `crypto_aead_aegis256_encrypt_detached` | `maclen_p = NULL`; `mlen ∈ MLEN` | [ ] |\n73\t| 6.22 | `crypto_aead_aegis256_decrypt_detached` | `m = NULL` verify-only; valid ⇒ `0`, tampered ⇒ `-1`; `mlen ∈ MLEN` | [ ] |\n74\t| 6.23 | `crypto_aead_aegis256_decrypt` / `_decrypt_detached` | `nsec` out-param `NULL` vs poisoned buffer | [ ] |\n75\t| 6.24 | `crypto_aead_aegis256_encrypt` / `_decrypt` | in-place `c == m` for `mlen ∈ {0, 1, 16, 17, 32, 1024}` | [ ] |\n76\t| 6.25 | `crypto_aead_aegis256_encrypt` / `_decrypt` | all-zero / all-`0xff` `k` and `npub`; `mlen ∈ {0, 16, 17}` | [ ] |\n77\t| 6.26 | `crypto_aead_aegis256_encrypt` | fixed AEGIS-256 KAT vectors | [ ] |\n78\t| **aes256gcm** — unavailable in this build (`is_available() == 0`; all ops `-1`/`ENOSYS`) | | | |\n79\t| 6.27 | `crypto_aead_aes256gcm_is_available` | no options; assert returns exactly `0` in the no-`HAVE_*` CMake configuration. Every other row in this block is conditioned on that | [ ] |\n80\t| 6.28 | `crypto_aead_aes256gcm_keybytes` / `_nsecbytes` / `_npubbytes` / `_abytes` / `_messagebytes_max` / `_statebytes` | still functional despite unavailability; assert `32 / 0 / 12 / 16 / MIN(SIZE_MAX-16, 16*(2^32-2))` and `_statebytes() == (sizeof(state)+15) & ~15` (multiple of 16, non-zero) | [ ] |\n81\t| 6.29 | `crypto_aead_aes256gcm_keygen` | still functional; fills 32 bytes; two calls differ | [ ] |\n82\t| 6.30 | `crypto_aead_aes256gcm_encrypt` | `mlen ∈ MLEN` × `adlen ∈ ADLEN` × `clen_p ∈ {NULL, non-NULL}`, `nsec = NULL`; **every** case ⇒ `-1`, `errno == ENOSYS`, `*clen_p` left at its pre-call poison value, `c` untouched | [ ] |\n83\t| 6.31 | `crypto_aead_aes256gcm_encrypt_detached` | `mlen ∈ MLEN` × `adlen ∈ ADLEN` × `maclen_p ∈ {NULL, non-NULL}`; all ⇒ `-1`/`ENOSYS`, `*maclen_p` poison preserved, `c`/`mac` untouched | [ ] |\n84\t| 6.32 | `crypto_aead_aes256gcm_decrypt` | `clen ∈ {0, 1, 15, 16, 17, 48}` (both below and above ABYTES) × `adlen ∈ ADLEN` × `mlen_p ∈ {NULL, non-NULL}`; all ⇒ `-1`/`ENOSYS`, `*mlen_p` poison preserved | [ ] |\n85\t| 6.33 | `crypto_aead_aes256gcm_decrypt_detached` | `clen ∈ MLEN` × `adlen ∈ ADLEN`, `m ∈ {NULL, non-NULL}`; all ⇒ `-1`/`ENOSYS`; `m` not even zeroed | [ ] |\n86\t| 6.34 | `crypto_aead_aes256gcm_beforenm` | 16-byte-aligned `crypto_aead_aes256gcm_state` (heap via `sodium_malloc` and stack via `CRYPTO_ALIGN(16)`), valid 32-byte `k`; ⇒ `-1`/`ENOSYS`, state left uninitialised | [ ] |\n87\t| 6.35 | `crypto_aead_aes256gcm_encrypt_afternm` | state from a failed `_beforenm` (the only kind obtainable); `mlen ∈ MLEN` × `adlen ∈ ADLEN` × `clen_p ∈ {NULL, non-NULL}`; all ⇒ `-1`/`ENOSYS` | [ ] |\n88\t| 6.36 | `crypto_aead_aes256gcm_encrypt_detached_afternm` | same state; `mlen ∈ MLEN` × `adlen ∈ ADLEN` × `maclen_p ∈ {NULL, non-NULL}`;