{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01Grz8KEXHAW1ebUVNiDwMby","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t## Area 6 — crypto_aead / secretbox / secretstream\n2\t\n3\tScope: `c_src/libsodium/crypto_aead/{aegis128l,aegis256,aes256gcm,chacha20poly1305,xchacha20poly1305}`,\n4\t`c_src/libsodium/crypto_secretbox/**`, `c_src/libsodium/crypto_secretstream/xchacha20poly1305/**`\n5\tand the matching public headers in `c_src/libsodium/include/sodium/`.\n6\t\n7\t### Build-configuration facts that drive this table\n8\t\n9\t* The CMake build defines **no** `HAVE_*` macros. Therefore:\n10\t  * `aegis128l`/`aegis256` `implementation` stays `&aegis128l_soft_implementation` /\n11\t    `&aegis256_soft_implementation` (the `HAVE_ARMCRYPTO` / `HAVE_AVXINTRIN_H`+`HAVE_WMMINTRIN_H`\n12\t    blocks in `_crypto_aead_aegis*_pick_best_implementation()` are compiled out), so the portable\n13\t    `aegis*_soft.c` path is the only one reachable. Behaviour (return codes) is identical either way.\n14\t  * The whole `#if !((HAVE_ARMCRYPTO && NATIVE_LITTLE_ENDIAN) || (HAVE_TMMINTRIN_H && HAVE_WMMINTRIN_H))`\n15\t    block at `crypto_aead/aes256gcm/aead_aes256gcm.c:50-157` **is** compiled, i.e. the **stub**\n16\t    family is what links. `crypto_aead_aes256gcm_is_available()` returns **0**, and *every* other\n17\t    aes256gcm entry point (`_encrypt`, `_encrypt_detached`, `_decrypt`, `_decrypt_detached`,\n18\t    `_beforenm`, `_encrypt_afternm`, `_encrypt_detached_afternm`, `_decrypt_afternm`,\n19\t    `_decrypt_detached_afternm`) unconditionally sets `errno = ENOSYS` (aliased to `ENXIO` if\n20\t    `ENOSYS` is undefined) and returns **-1**, *without touching* `*clen_p` / `*mlen_p` /\n21\t    `*maclen_p` and without reading any input buffer. Rows 6.30–6.39 below.\n22\t  * `crypto_aead_aes256gcm_keybytes/nsecbytes/npubbytes/abytes/statebytes/messagebytes_max/keygen`\n23\t    live *outside* that `#if`, so they still work normally even though the cipher is unavailable.\n24\t* `sodium_misuse()` (`sodium/core.c:192`) calls the registered misuse handler if any and then\n25\t  `abort()`s. It **never returns**. Every `mlen > *_MESSAGEBYTES_MAX` overflow guard in this area\n26\t  (except the aegis `*_decrypt_detached` ones, which `return -1`) goes through `sodium_misuse()`,\n27\t  i.e. the \"expected C result\" is *process abort*, not an error code. These branches are marked\n28\t  `abort()` below and are unreachable on 64-bit hosts for realistically-sized inputs (they are\n29\t  all tagged `LCOV_EXCL_LINE` upstream).\n30\t* Relevant constants: `aegis128l` KEY 16 / NPUB 16 / **ABYTES 32** / NSEC 0;\n31\t  `aegis256` KEY 32 / NPUB 32 / **ABYTES 32** / NSEC 0;\n32\t  `aes256gcm` KEY 32 / NPUB 12 / ABYTES 16 / NSEC 0;\n33\t  `chacha20poly1305` KEY 32 / **NPUB 8** / ABYTES 16 / NSEC 0;\n34\t  `chacha20poly1305_ietf` KEY 32 / **NPUB 12** / ABYTES 16 / NSEC 0;\n35\t  `xchacha20poly1305_ietf` KEY 32 / **NPUB 24** / ABYTES 16 / NSEC 0;\n36\t  `secretbox` KEY 32 / NONCE 24 / MAC 16 / BOXZERO 16 / **ZERO 32**;\n37\t  `secretstream_xchacha20poly1305` KEY 32 / HEADER 24 / **ABYTES 17** (= 1 + 16),\n38\t  `TAG_MESSAGE 0x00`, `TAG_PUSH 0x01`, `TAG_REKEY 0x02`, `TAG_FINAL 0x03`.\n39\t* `MESSAGEBYTES_MAX`: `aegis128l`/`aegis256` = `MIN(SIZE_MAX-32, 2^61-1)`;\n40\t  `aes256gcm` = `MIN(SIZE_MAX-16, 16*(2^32-2))`; `chacha20poly1305` = `SIZE_MAX-16`;\n41\t  `chacha20poly1305_ietf` = `MIN(SIZE_MAX-16, 64*(2^32-1))`;\n42\t  `xchacha20poly1305_ietf` = `SIZE_MAX-16`;\n43\t  `secretbox_xsalsa20poly1305` / `secretbox_xchacha20poly1305` = `stream_MESSAGEBYTES_MAX - 16`;\n44\t  `secretstream` = `MIN(SIZE_MAX-17, 64*(2^32-2))`.\n45\t* `nsec` is `NSECBYTES == 0` for every AEAD here. Every implementation does `(void) nsec;`\n46\t  (`aead_aegis128l.c:115,136`, `aead_aegis256.c:115,135`, `aead_chacha20poly1305.c:38,122,212,293`,\n47\t  `aead_xchacha20poly1305.c:40,108`) — i.e. `nsec` is *always ignored*, `NULL` and non-`NULL` are\n48\t  indistinguishable, and it is never written on the decrypt side. There is no rejection branch for it.\n49\t* The combined `*_encrypt`/`*_decrypt` wrappers are the only place `clen_p`/`mlen_p` are written;\n50\t  they are all `if (ptr != NULL)`-guarded, so a `NULL` out-length pointer is **legal** and simply\n51\t  suppresses the store. Same for `maclen_p` in `*_encrypt_detached` and `outlen_p`/`mlen_p`/`tag_p`\n52\t  in secretstream. No rejection branch — but the *value* written on failure is load-bearing (0).\n53\t\n54\t### ERROR-SURFACE table\n55\t\n56\t| # | function | trigger (exact invalid input/condition) | expected C result |\n57\t|---|----------|------------------------------------------|-------------------|\n58\t| 6.1 | `crypto_aead_aegis128l_encrypt` | `mlen > crypto_aead_aegis128l_MESSAGEBYTES_MAX` (`aead_aegis128l.c:69`) | `sodium_misuse()` → misuse handler then `abort()`; never returns |\n59\t| 6.2 | `crypto_aead_aegis128l_encrypt_detached` | `mlen > crypto_aead_aegis128l_MESSAGEBYTES_MAX` (`aead_aegis128l.c:119`) | `sodium_misuse()` → `abort()`. NB `*maclen_p = 32` has *already* been stored at line 117 before the check |\n60\t| 6.3 | `crypto_aead_aegis128l_encrypt_detached` | `adlen > crypto_aead_aegis128l_MESSAGEBYTES_MAX` (`aead_aegis128l.c:120`) | `sodium_misuse()` → `abort()` |\n61\t| 6.4 | `crypto_aead_aegis128l_decrypt` | `clen < 32` (`clen < crypto_aead_aegis128l_ABYTES`, guard at `aead_aegis128l.c:92` not taken) — includes `clen == 0` and `clen == 31` | returns `-1`; if `mlen_p != NULL` then `*mlen_p = 0`; `m` untouched; detached path never entered |\n62\t| 6.5 | `crypto_aead_aegis128l_decrypt` | `clen >= 32` but tag (last 32 bytes) does not verify — flipped ciphertext bit, flipped tag bit, wrong `k`, wrong `npub`, wrong/absent `ad` | returns `-1` (propagated from `_decrypt_detached`); if `mlen_p != NULL` then `*mlen_p = 0`; `m[0 .. clen-32)` is zeroed by `aegis128l_soft.c:249` |\n63\t| 6.6 | `crypto_aead_aegis128l_decrypt_detached` | `clen > crypto_aead_aegis128l_MESSAGEBYTES_M