,111,112,127,128,129} | [ ] |\n17\t| 4.2 | `crypto_auth_verify` | generic verify; good 32-byte tag ⇒ `0`; tag with one flipped bit ⇒ `-1`; identical results to `crypto_auth_hmacsha512256_verify` | [ ] |\n18\t| 4.3 | `crypto_auth_keygen` | fills exactly `crypto_auth_KEYBYTES` = 32 bytes from `randombytes_buf`; two successive calls differ; no bytes written past index 31 | [ ] |\n19\t| 4.4 | `crypto_auth_primitive` | returns the literal `\"hmacsha512256\"` (`crypto_auth_PRIMITIVE`) | [ ] |\n20\t| 4.5 | `crypto_auth_bytes` / `crypto_auth_keybytes` | return 32 / 32, matching the macros `crypto_auth_BYTES` = `crypto_auth_hmacsha512256_BYTES` and `crypto_auth_KEYBYTES` = `crypto_auth_hmacsha512256_KEYBYTES` | [ ] |\n21\t| 4.6 | `crypto_auth.h` surface shape | the generic `crypto_auth` API deliberately exposes **no** state type, **no** `crypto_auth_statebytes`, and **no** init/update/final — streaming is only reachable through the primitive-specific names. Port must not invent a generic streaming API | [ ] |\n22\t| 4.7 | `crypto_auth_hmacsha256` | one-shot; 32-byte key; `inlen = 0` (empty message) | [ ] |\n23\t| 4.8 | `crypto_auth_hmacsha256` | one-shot; 32-byte key; `inlen = 1` | [ ] |\n24\t| 4.9 | `crypto_auth_hmacsha256` | one-shot; 32-byte key; `inlen = 55` (inner hash: last block has exactly 9 bytes for pad+length after the 64-byte ipad block) | [ ] |\n25\t| 4.10 | `crypto_auth_hmacsha256` | one-shot; 32-byte key; `inlen = 56` (pad spills into an extra SHA-256 block) | [ ] |\n26\t| 4.11 | `crypto_auth_hmacsha256` | one-shot; 32-byte key; `inlen = 63` | [ ] |\n27\t| 4.12 | `crypto_auth_hmacsha256` | one-shot; 32-byte key; `inlen = 64` (exactly one SHA-256 block after the ipad block) | [ ] |\n28\t| 4.13 | `crypto_auth_hmacsha256` | one-shot; 32-byte key; `inlen = 65` | [ ] |\n29\t| 4.14 | `crypto_auth_hmacsha256` | one-shot; 32-byte key; `inlen = 111` | [ ] |\n30\t| 4.15 | `crypto_auth_hmacsha256` | one-shot; 32-byte key; `inlen = 112` | [ ] |\n31\t| 4.16 | `crypto_auth_