{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01XnbY5KJM1rV2iSeUZVE6AM","type":"tool_result","content":"1:## Area 7 — scalarmult / sign / box / kx / kdf / kem\n20:### Constants resolved (LP64, CMake build with no `HAVE_*` macros)\n47:### Global observations about the error surface\n61:### ERROR-SURFACE table\n65:| 7.1 | `crypto_scalarmult_curve25519` (`scalarmult_curve25519.c:15`) → `crypto_scalarmult_curve25519_ref10` (`x25519_ref10.c:106`) | `p` = 32 zero bytes (blocklist entry 0, \"0\", order 4) | `-1`. `has_small_order(p) != 0` → `mult` returns `-1` → wrapper returns `-1` at `:22`. `q` **not** written (early reject before the ladder). |\n66:| 7.2 | same | `p` = `01 00 … 00` (blocklist entry 1, \"1\", order 1) | `-1`, `q` untouched. |\n67:| 7.3 | same | `p` = `e0 eb 7a 7c 3b 41 b8 ae 16 56 e3 fa f1 9f c4 6a da 09 8d eb 9c 32 b1 fd 86 62 05 16 5f 49 b8 00` (order 8) | `-1`, `q` untouched. |\n68:| 7.4 | same | `p` = `5f 9c 95 bc a3 50 8c 24 b1 d0 b1 55 9c 83 ef 5b 04 44 5c c4 58 1c 8e 86 d8 22 4e dd d0 9f 11 57` (order 8) | `-1`, `q` untouched. |\n69:| 7.5 | same | `p` = `ec ff … ff 7f` (`p-1`, order 2) | `-1`, `q` untouched. |\n70:| 7.6 | same | `p` = `ed ff … ff 7f` (`= p`, i.e. the non-canonical encoding of 0, order 4) | `-1`, `q` untouched. |\n71:| 7.7 | same | `p` = `ee ff … ff 7f` (`= p+1`, non-canonical encoding of 1, order 1) | `-1`, `q` untouched. |\n72:| 7.8 | same | any of 7.1–7.7 with bit 255 set (`p[31] |= 0x80`) | `-1` still. The comparison masks `s[31] & 0x7f` (`x25519_ref10.c:63`), so the high bit cannot be used to bypass the blocklist. |\n73:| 7.9 | `crypto_scalarmult_curve25519` — post-hoc all-zero-output guard (`scalarmult_curve25519.c:24-27`) | `implementation->mult` returns `0` but the 32 output bytes are all zero (`d == 0`) | `-1` via `-(1 & ((d - 1) >> 8))`. **Unreachable with ref10** because every low-order input is already rejected by 7.1–7.8 — but it is a distinct branch that must be preserved (it is the defence-in-depth guard for a hypothetical optimised backend). `q` **has** been written (all zeros) when this fires. |\n74:| 7.10 | `crypto_scalarmult` (`crypto_scalarmult.c:17`) | any of 7.1–7.9 | `-1`. Thin alias for `crypto_scalarmult_curve25519`; adds nothing. |\n75:| 7.11 | `crypto_scalarmult_curve25519_base` (`scalarmult_curve25519.c:31`) | **none** — including `n` = 32 zero bytes, `n` = `ff…ff`, `n` = `L` | Always `0`. `crypto_scalarmult_curve25519_ref10_base` clamps (`t[0] &= 248; t[31] &= 127; t[31] |= 64`) into `t = q` and unconditionally `return 0` (`x25519_ref10.c:191`). There is **no** all-zero check and **no** small-order check on this path (the wrapper is bypassed). `n = 0` yields the valid X25519 pk for scalar `2^254`. |\n76:| 7.12 | `crypto_scalarmult_base` (`crypto_scalarmult.c:11`) | **none** | Always `0`. Alias for 7.11. |\n77:| 7.13 | `crypto_scalarmult_ed25519` / `_noclamp` (`scalarmult_ed25519_ref10.c:39`) | `p` is a non-canonical point encoding: `p[31] & 0x7f == 0x7f`, `p[1..30] == 0xff`, `p[0] >= 0xed` (i.e. `y >= 2^255 - 19`) | `-1` (`ge25519_is_canonical(p) == 0`). Early reject: `q` untouched. |\n78:| 7.14 | same | `p` decodes to a `y` for which `x^2 = (y^2-1)/(dy^2+1)` is not a square → `ge25519_frombytes(&P, p) != 0` (e.g. `p = 02 00 … 00`) | `-1`. Early reject, `q` untouched. |\n79:| 7.15 | same | `p` = `01 00 … 00` (Edwards identity) → `ge25519_has_small_order(&P) != 0` | `-1`. Early reject, `q` untouched. |\n80:| 7.16 | same | `p` is any of the other 7 torsion encodings (`00 00 … 00` order 4; `ec ff … 7f` order 2; the two order-4 and four order-8 points) → `ge25519_has_small_order` | `-1`. Note `ge25519_has_small_order` tests `X==0 ∨ Y==0 ∨ Z==0 ∨ Y·√-1 == X ∨ Y·√-1 == -X` (`ed25519_ref10.c:1173-1189`). Early reject, `q` untouched. |\n81:| 7.17 | same | `p` is a valid, canonical, non-small-order point that is **not** in the prime-order subgroup (order `2L`, `4L` or `8L`; obtain by adding a torsion point to a legitimate pk) → `ge25519_is_on_main_subgroup(&P) == 0` | `-1`. Early reject, `q` untouched. |\n82:| 7.18 | `crypto_scalarmult_ed25519` (clamped, `:53`) | `n` = 32 zero bytes, valid `p` | `-1` via `sodium_is_zero(n, 32)`. Note clamping makes the *effective* scalar `2^254`, so `_is_inf(q)` is false and it is the `sodium_is_zero(n,…)` disjunct that fires. **Late reject: `q` already holds the (valid, non-identity) result bytes.** |\n83:| 7.19 | `crypto_scalarmult_ed25519_noclamp` (`:53`) | `n` = 32 zero bytes, valid `p` | `-1`. Both disjuncts fire: `q` is the identity so `_crypto_scalarmult_ed25519_is_inf(q) != 0`, and `sodium_is_zero(n,32)`. **Late reject: `q` holds `01 00 … 00`.** |\n84:| 7.20 | `crypto_scalarmult_ed25519_noclamp` | `n` = `L` little-endian (`ed d3 f5 5c 1a 63 12 58 d6 9c f7 a2 de f9 de 14 00 … 00 10`), valid `p` | `-1` via `_is_inf(q)` only (`sodium_is_zero(n)` is false). **Late reject: `q` = `01 00 … 00`.** Same for any `n ≡ 0 (mod L)` whose bit 255 is clear. |\n85:| 7.21 | `crypto_scalarmult_ed25519_noclamp` | `n = L + 2^255` (bit 255 set) | `-1`. `t[31] &= 127` (`:49`) clears bit 255 unconditionally on **both** the clamped and noclamp paths, so this reduces to 7.20. Bit 255 of `n` is never honoured. |\n86:| 7.22 | `crypto_scalarmult_ed25519_base` (`_crypto_scalarmult_ed25519_base`, `:91`) | `n` = 32 zero bytes | `-1` via `sodium_is_zero(n, 32)` (clamping means `_is_inf` is false). **Late reject: `q` holds the valid point for scalar `2^254`.** |\n87:| 7.23 | `crypto_scalarmult_ed25519_base_noclamp` (`:91`) | `n` = 32 zero bytes | `-1`; both `_is_inf(q)` and `sodium_is_zero(n,32)` fire. `q` = `01 00 … 00`. |\n88:| 7.24 | `crypto_scalarmult_ed25519_base_noclamp` | `n = L` (or any nonzero `n ≡ 0 mod L`, bit 255 cleared) | `-1` via `_is_inf(q)` only. `q` = `01 00 … 00`. |\n89:| 7.25 | all four `crypto_scalarmult_ed25519*` | **shared side-effect contract**, not a trigger | On the *early* rejects (7.13–7.17) `q` is untouched; on the *late* rejects (7.18–7.24) `q` has been fully writt