 only swept the *keystream* forms. Added prefix consistency for every `_xor` and `_xor_ic` form at four `ic` values each.\n- The `_xor_ic(ic=k)` == tail-of-long-`_xor`-at-block-k identity was pinned against the independent `_xor` entry point for salsa20/xsalsa20/chacha20/xchacha20, but for `chacha20_ietf` it was only cross-checked against `_xor_ic(0)`. Added the real `_xor`-based check for `_ietf_xor_ic` and `_ietf_ext_xor_ic` (ic ∈ {0,1,2,3,7,8} × all of `L` × 8 shapes).\n- Error row 5.18 for the generic wrapper, with the guarded-mapping technique.\n\n### errors_5.md — `status` column added to all 29 rows\nHeader and separator now match `errors_1.md` exactly. Tally: **13 verified, 11 dead-branch-on-this-build, 4 checked-via-guard-only, 1 unreachable-from-public-API**.\n\nNon-`verified` rows and why:\n- `dead-branch-on-this-build` (11): **5.1–5.6, 5.11, 5.12** — the six `> crypto_stream_chacha20_MESSAGEBYTES_MAX` comparisons plus the two xchacha20 inheritances; `MESSAGEBYTES_MAX == UINT64_MAX` on LP64 and the parameter is `unsigned long long`, so the predicate is provably false. Pinned as \"no abort\" via the guarded mapping (SIGSEGV, not SIGABRT). **5.20–5.22** — `COMPILER_ASSERT` is `(void) sizeof(char[(X)?1:-1])`, compile-time only, no runtime branch emitted.\n- `checked-via-guard-only` (4): **5.7, 5.10** (`_ietf`/`_ietf_xor` reject `clen/mlen > 2^38`) and **5.9** (the `2^32 - ceil(mlen/64)` underflow hole for `mlen > 2^38`) and **5.15** (`_ext` accepting `> 2^38`). All need a 256 GiB buffer, so they are driven with an over-limit length against a 1 MiB mapping backed by a `PROT_NONE` guard page. I re-verified in `stream_chacha20.c` that the `sodium_misuse()` test is the *first* statement of each function and no argument pointer is dereferenced before it, so the pointer is never touched on the abort path; the accept path deterministically SIGSEGVs, which is what separates \"guard fired\" (`sig:6`) from \"guard did not fire\" (`sig:11`).\n- `unreachable-from-public-API` (1): **5.19** — salsa2012/salsa208 counter wraparound requires `ceil(mlen/64) > 2^64`, i.e. `mlen > 2^70`, which is not representable in `unsigned long long`. There is no `_xor_ic` entry point for these two primitives, so the counter always starts at 0 and the condition cannot be produced at all.\n\nThe `verified` set includes all the rows the artifact flags as interesting: 5.8 (the reachable 32-bit-counter guard, tested at the exact accepted boundary `ic + ceil(mlen/64) == 2^32` and one past it with real small messages), 5.13/5.14 (chacha20 silent 64-bit wrap and the correct 32→64 carry), 5.16 (`_ietf_ext_xor_ic`'s silent counter overflow into nonce word 0, checked against a nonce-word-0-incremented counter-0 run), 5.17 (salsa20/xsalsa20 silent 64-bit wrap, checked by asserting the post-wrap block equals the counter-0 block).\n\n### Divergences\n**None.** C and Rust agreed on every one of the added cases, including all the guard/abort process outcomes, so no file under `/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-RqaJY6/translation/src/` was modified. `c_src/` untouched. I also diffed the exported symbol sets: all 60 `crypto_stream*` symbols are present and identical in both `.so` files.\n\n`build-phaseA.sh` re-run: area 5 now reports **89 rows / 89 checked**; totals 1170 rows / 745 checked (CONFIGS.md) and 774 error rows (ERRORS.md).\n\nOne note for whoever aggregates: the two existing files use a local `outcomes()` helper for the abort rows rather than `eq_abort` directly. It is a strict superset — it calls `in_child` on both libraries, asserts the statuses match (exactly what `eq_abort` does) and additionally pins the specific expected signal — so I left it alone rather than churn working code; the new file uses `eq_abort` directly.","usage":{"total_tokens":133742,"tool_uses":27,"duration_ms":392405},"uuid":"a278c912-e240-426f-a759-9a97b485085e","session_id":"b566403e-4c11-4a9b-ad3a-d43b1f08afd2"}
