Rust: {}\",\n345\t            c.len(),\n346\t            r.len(),\n347\t            hex(c),\n348\t            hex(r)\n349\t        );\n350\t    }\n351\t}\n352\t\n353\t#[track_caller]\n354\tpub fn eqi(what: &str, c: c_int, r: c_int) {\n355\t    assert_eq!(c, r, \"{what}: return code mismatch (C {c}, Rust {r})\");\n356\t}\n357\t\n358\tpub fn hex(b: &[u8]) -> String {\n359\t    const MAX: usize = 160;\n360\t    let mut s = String::new();\n361\t    for (i, x) in b.iter().enumerate() {\n362\t        if i == MAX {\n363\t            s.push_str(\"...\");\n364\t            break;\n365\t        }\n366\t        s.push_str(&format!(\"{x:02x}\"));\n367\t    }\n368\t    s\n369\t}\n370\t\n371\t// ------------------------------------------------------------------- errno\n372\t\n373\textern \"C\" {\n374\t    fn __errno_location() -> *mut c_int;\n375\t    fn fork() -> c_int;\n376\t    fn waitpid(pid: c_int, status: *mut c_int, options: c_int) -> c_int;\n377\t    fn _exit(code: c_int) -> !;\n378\t    fn setrlimit(resource: c_int, rlim: *const RLimit) -> c_int;\n379\t}\n380\t\n381\t#[repr(C)]\n382\tstruct RLimit {\n383\t    cur: u64,\n384\t    max: u64,\n385\t}\n386\tconst RLIMIT_CORE: c_int = 4;\n387\t\n388\tpub fn errno() -> c_int {\n389\t    unsafe { *__errno_location() }\n390\t}\n391\tpub fn set_errno(v: c_int) {\n392\t    unsafe { *__errno_location() = v }\n393\t}\n394\t\n395\tpub const EINVAL: c_int = 22;\n396\tpub const ERANGE: c_int = 34;\n397\tpub const ENOMEM: c_int = 12;\n398\tpub const EPERM: c_int = 1;\n399\t\n400\t/// Run `f` in a forked child and return the raw `waitpid` status.\n401\t///\n402\t/// Used for the `sodium_misuse()` / `abort()` paths, which cannot be observed\n403\t/// in-process.  The child `_exit(0)`s if `f` returns normally, so a normal exit\n404\t/// and a fatal signal are clearly distinguishable.\n405\tpub fn in_child<F: FnOnce()>(f: F) -> c_int {\n406\t    unsafe {\n407\t        let pid = fork();\n408\t        assert!(pid >= 0, \"fork failed\");\n409\t        if pid == 0 {\n410\t            // No core dumps: the abort() paths are expected, and dumping cores\n411\t            // makes the error-path tests hundreds of times slower.\n412\t            setrlimit(RLIMIT_CORE, &RLimit { cur: 0, max: 0 });\n413\t            f();\n414\t            _exit(0);\n415\t        }\n416\t        let mut st: c_int = 0;\n417\t        assert!(waitpid(pid, &mut st, 0) == pid);\n418\t        st\n419\t    }\n420\t}\n421\t\n422\t/// Human-readable classification of a `waitpid` status: `\"exit:N\"` or `\"sig:N\"`.\n423\tpub fn status_str(st: c_int) -> String {\n424\t    if st & 0x7f == 0 {\n425\t        format!(\"exit:{}\", (st >> 8) & 0xff)\n426\t    } else {\n427\t        format!(\"sig:{}\", st & 0x7f)\n428\t    }\n429\t}\n430\t\n431\t/// Differentially compare the *process outcome* of a pair of calls that may\n432\t/// abort (`sodium_misuse`).\n433\t#[track_caller]\n434\tpub fn eq_abort<FC: FnOnce(), FR: FnOnce()>(what: &str, c: FC, r: FR) {\n435\t    let sc = in_child(c);\n436\t    let sr = in_child(r);\n437\t    assert_eq!(\n438\t        status_str(sc),\n439\t        status_str(sr),\n440\t        \"{what}: process outcome mismatch\"\n441\t    );\n442\t}\n443\t\n444\t/// A guard byte pattern used to prove neither implementation writes out of\n445\t/// bounds; allocate `len + PAD` and check the tail afterwards.\n446\tpub const PAD: usize = 32;\n447\t\n448\tpub fn padded(len: usize) -> Vec<u8> {\n449\t    let mut v = vec![0u8; len + PAD];\n450\t    for (i, b) in v[len..].iter_mut().enumerate() {\n451\t        *b = 0xA5u8.wrapping_add(i as u8);\n452\t    }\n453\t    v\n454\t}\n455\t\n456\t#[track_caller]\n457\tpub fn check_pad(what: &str, v: &[u8], len: usize) {\n458\t    for (i, b) in v[len..].iter().enumerate() {\n459\t        assert_eq!(\n460\t            *b,\n461\t            0xA5u8.wrapping_add(i as u8),\n462\t            \"{what}: out-of-bounds write at +{i} past {len}\"\n463\t        );\n464\t    }\n465\t}\n466\t"}]},"parent_tool_