`crypto_scalarmult_ristretto255_BYTES` / `_SCALARBYTES` | `32` / `32` |\n28\t| `crypto_sign_ed25519_BYTES` / `_SEEDBYTES` / `_PUBLICKEYBYTES` / `_SECRETKEYBYTES` | `64` / `32` / `32` / `64` |\n29\t| `crypto_sign_ed25519_MESSAGEBYTES_MAX` = `SODIUM_SIZE_MAX - 64` | `0xFFFF_FFFF_FFFF_FFBF` = `2^64 - 65` |\n30\t| `crypto_sign_BYTES/SEEDBYTES/PUBLICKEYBYTES/SECRETKEYBYTES/PRIMITIVE` | `64` / `32` / `32` / `64` / `\"ed25519\"` (aliases ed25519) |\n31\t| `crypto_box_*_SEEDBYTES` / `_PUBLICKEYBYTES` / `_SECRETKEYBYTES` / `_BEFORENMBYTES` | `32` / `32` / `32` / `32` (both xsalsa and xchacha) |\n32\t| `crypto_box_*_NONCEBYTES` / `_MACBYTES` | `24` / `16` (both) |\n33\t| `crypto_box_curve25519xsalsa20poly1305_BOXZEROBYTES` / `_ZEROBYTES` | `16` / `16 + 16 = 32` (NaCl-style only; xchacha has neither) |\n34\t| `crypto_box_SEALBYTES` / `crypto_box_curve25519xchacha20poly1305_SEALBYTES` | `32 + 16 = 48` / `32 + 16 = 48` |\n35\t| `crypto_box_*_MESSAGEBYTES_MAX` | `crypto_secretbox_*_MESSAGEBYTES_MAX` = `SODIUM_SIZE_MAX - 16` = `2^64 - 17` |\n36\t| `crypto_box_PRIMITIVE` | `\"curve25519xsalsa20poly1305\"` |\n37\t| `crypto_kx_PUBLICKEYBYTES` / `_SECRETKEYBYTES` / `_SEEDBYTES` / `_SESSIONKEYBYTES` / `_PRIMITIVE` | `32` / `32` / `32` / `32` / `\"x25519blake2b\"` |\n38\t| `crypto_kdf_blake2b_BYTES_MIN` / `_BYTES_MAX` / `_CONTEXTBYTES` / `_KEYBYTES` | `16` / `64` / `8` / `32` |\n39\t| `crypto_kdf_BYTES_MIN` / `_BYTES_MAX` / `_CONTEXTBYTES` / `_KEYBYTES` / `_PRIMITIVE` | `16` / `64` / `8` / `32` / `\"blake2b\"` (aliases blake2b) |\n40\t| `crypto_kdf_hkdf_sha256_KEYBYTES` / `_BYTES_MIN` / `_BYTES_MAX` | `32` / `0` / `0xff * 32 = 8160` |\n41\t| `crypto_kdf_hkdf_sha512_KEYBYTES` / `_BYTES_MIN` / `_BYTES_MAX` | `64` / `0` / `0xff * 64 = 16320` |\n42\t| `crypto_kem_mlkem768_PUBLICKEYBYTES` / `_SECRETKEYBYTES` / `_CIPHERTEXTBYTES` / `_SHAREDSECRETBYTES` / `_SEEDBYTES` | `1184` / `2400` / `1088` / `32` / `64` |\n43\t| `crypto_kem_xwing_PUBLICKEYBYTES` / `_SECRETKEYBYTES` / `_CIPHERTEXTBYTES` / `_SHAREDSECRETBYTES` / `_SEEDBYTES` | `1216` / `32` / `1120` / `32` / `32` |\n44\t| `crypto_kem_*` generic / `_PRIMITIVE` | aliases xwing / `\"xwing\"` |\n45\t| mlkem768 internals | `Q=3329`, `N=256`, `K=3`, `POLYBYTES=384`, `POLYVECBYTES=1152`, `POLYVECCOMPRESSEDBYTES_DU=960`, `POLYCOMPRESSEDBYTES_DV=128` |\n46\t\n47\t### Global observations about the error surface\n48\t\n49\t- **`-1` is the only error code in area 7.** No function here sets a distinguishing errno except the two kdf bound checks (`kdf_blake2b.c:45`, `kdf_hkdf_sha256.c:66`, `kdf_hkdf_sha512.c:66` set `errno = EINVAL` before `return -1`). Everything else returns bare `-1` / `0`.\n50\t- **`sodium_misuse()` sites (abort, not an error return):** `crypto_box_easy_afternm`, `crypto_box_easy`, `crypto_box_seal` (`crypto_box_easy.c:45,57`, `crypto_box_seal.c:34`), the two xchacha equivalents plus `..._seal` (`box_curve25519xchacha20poly1305.c:94,106`, `box_seal_curve25519xchacha20poly1305.c:40`), and `crypto_kx_{client,server}_session_keys` when **both** `rx` and `tx` are NULL (`crypto_kx.c:52,93`).\n51\t- **Output-buffer aliasing on failure is load-bearing.** In `scalarmult_ed25519_ref10.c` and `scalarmult_ristretto255_ref10.c` the clamped scalar is staged in `q` itself (`unsigned char *t = q;`), and in `x25519_ref10.c:176` `crypto_scalarmult_curve25519_ref10_base` does the same. So on the *late* rejection paths (`_is_inf` / `sodium_is_zero(q,…)`) the caller's `q` has already been fully overwritten with the result; on the *early* rejection paths (`ge25519_is_canonical` / `ristretto255_frombytes` / `has_small_order(p)`) `q` is untouched. A Rust port must reproduce which is which.\n52\t- **`crypto_scalarmult_curve25519` has two independent guards**: the ref10 `has_small_order(p)` blocklist (`x25519_ref10.c:106`) *and* the post-hoc all-zero-output check `return -(1 & ((d - 1) >> 8));` (`scalarmult_curve25519.c:24-27`). Note `d` is `volatile unsigned char`; with `d == 0`, `d - 1` promotes to `int` `-1`, `-1 >> 8 == -1`, `1 & -1 == 1`, so the return is `-1`. With `d != 0` the expression is `-(1 & 0) == 0`.\n53\t- **`crypto_scalarmult_curve25519_base` bypasses the wrapper entirely** — it calls `crypto_scalarmult_curve25519_ref10_implementation.mult_base` directly (`scalarmult_curve25519.c:33-35`), so there is **no** all-zero-output check on the `_base` path, and `crypto_scalarmult_curve25519_ref10_base` unconditionally `return 0`. `_base` can never fail.\n54\t- **ed25519 signature verification is cofactored**: the last line of `_crypto_sign_ed25519_verify_detached` is `return ge25519_has_small_order(&check) - 1;` — success (`0`) requires `check = R - (sB - hA)` to be a *small-order* point, not strictly the identity. This accepts signatures off by any torsion component.\n55\t- **mlkem768 decapsulation never fails.** `mlkem768_ref_dec` always `return 0` and uses *implicit rejection*: on re-encryption mismatch it constant-time-swaps in `SHAKE256(z || ct)`. There is no ciphertext validity check whatsoever. Hence the `if (crypto_kem_mlkem768_dec(...) != 0)` branch in `crypto_kem_xwing_dec` (`kem_xwing.c:188`) is dead (it is marked `LCOV_EXCL`).\n56\t- **mlkem768 `_enc`/`_enc_deterministic` is the only kem function with a real validity check**: `polyvec_is_canonical(&pkpv) == 0` → `-1` (`kem_mlkem768_ref.c:745-747`), i.e. any of the 768 unpacked 12-bit coefficients of `pk[0..1151]` being `>= 3329`.\n57\t- **Every public entry point is `__attribute__((nonnull))`.** NULL pointers are UB, never an error return — with the single, deliberate exception of `rx`/`tx` in `crypto_kx_{client,server}_session_keys`, which are explicitly NULL-checked and mutually substituted.\n58\t- **No `assert()` anywhere in area 7.** The only asserts are `COMPILER_ASSERT` (compile-time): `x25519_ref10.c:56` (blocklist has 7 entries), `crypto_box_easy.c:29`, `box_curve25519xchacha20poly1305.c:74-75`, `crypto_box_seal.c:60`, `box_seal_curve25519xchacha20poly1305.c:68-69`, `crypto_kx.c:26-27,57,98`.\n59\t- **`has_small_order` ignores the top bit of `p`**: the final byte is compared as `(s[31] & 0x7f)` (`x25519_ref10.c:63`), so setting bit 255 on a blocklisted encoding does **not** evade the guard.\n60\t\n61\t### ERROR-SURFACE table\n62\t\n63\t| # | function | trigger (exact invalid input/condition) | expected C result | status |\n64\t|---|----------|------------------------------------------|-------------------|-------|\n65\t| 7.1 | `crypto_scalarmult_curve25519` (`scalarmult_curve25519.c:15`) → `crypto_scalarmult_curve25519_ref10` (`x25519_ref10.c:106`) | `p` = 32 zero bytes (blocklist entry 0, \"0\", order 4) | `-1`. `has_small_order(p) != 0` → `mult` returns `-1` → wrapper returns `-1` at `:22`. `q` **not** written (early reject before the ladder). |\n66\t| 7.2 | same | `p` = `01 00 … 00` (blocklist entry 1, \"1\", order 1) | `-1`, `q` untouched. |\n67\t| 7.3 | same | `p` = `e0 eb 7a 7c 3b 41 b8 ae 16 56 e3 fa f1 9f c4 6a da 09 8d eb 9c 32 b1 fd 86 62 05 16 5f 49 b8 00` (order 8) | `-1`, `q` untouched. |\n68\t| 7.4 | same | `p` = `5f 9c 95 bc a3 50 8c 24 b1 d0 b1 55 9c 83 ef 5b 04 44 5c c4 58 1c 8e 86 d8 22 4e dd d0 9f 11 57` (order 8) | `-1`, `q` untouched. |\n69\t| 7.5 | same | `p` = `ec ff … ff 7f` (`p-1`, order 2) | `-1`, `q` untouched. |\n70\t| 7.6 | same | `p` = `ed ff … ff 7f` (`= p`, i.e. the non-canonical encoding of 0, order 4) | `-1`, `q` untouched. |\n71\t| 7.7 | same | `p` = `ee ff … ff 7f` (`= p+1`, non-canonical encoding of 1, order 1) | `-1`, `q` untouched. |\n72\t| 7.8 | same | any of 7.1–7.7 with bit 255 set (`p[31] |= 0x80`) | `-1` still. The comparison masks `s[31] & 0x7f` (`x25519_ref10.c:63`), so the high bit cannot be used to bypass the blocklist. |\n73\t| 7.9 | `crypto_scalarmult_curve25519` — post-hoc all-zero-output guard (`scalarmult_curve25519.c:24-27`) | `implementation->mult` returns `0` but the 32 output bytes are all zero (`d == 0`) | `-1` via `-(1 & ((d - 1) >> 8))`. **Unreachable with ref10** because every low-order input is already rejected by 7.1–7.8 — but it is a distinct branch that must be preserved (it is the defence-in-depth guard for a hypothetical optimised backend). `q` **has** been written (all zeros) when this fires. |\n74\t| 7.10 | `crypto_scalarmult` (`crypto_scalarmult.c:17`) | any of 7.1–7.9 | `-1`. Thin alias for `crypto_scalarmult_curve25519`; adds nothing. |\n75\t| 7.11 | `crypto_scalarmult_curve25519_base` (`scalarmult_curve25519.c:31`) | **none** — including `n` = 32 zero bytes, `n` = `ff…ff`, `n` = `L` | Always `0`. `crypto_scalarmult_curve25519_ref10_base` clamps (`t[0] &= 248; t[31] &= 127; t[31] |= 64`) into `t = q` and unconditionally `return 0` (`x25519_ref10.c:191`). There is **no** all-zero check and **no** small-order check on this path (the wrapper is bypassed). `n = 0` yields the valid X25519 pk for scalar `2^254`. |\n76\t| 7.12 | `crypto_scalarmult_base` (`crypto_scalarmult.c:11`) | **none** | Always `0`. Alias for 7.11. |\n77\t| 7.13 | `crypto_scalarmult_ed25519` / `_noclamp` (`scalarmult_ed25519_ref10.c:39`) | `p` is a non-canonical point encoding: `p[31] & 0x7f == 0x7f`, `p[1..30] == 0xff`, `p[0] >= 0xed` (i.e. `y >= 2^255 - 19`) | `-1` (`ge25519_is_canonical(p) == 0`). Early reject: `q` untouched. |\n78\t| 7.14 | same | `p` decodes to a `y` for which `x^2 = (y^2-1)/(dy^2+1)` is not a square → `ge25519_frombytes(&P, p) != 0` (e.g. `p = 02 00 … 00`) | `-1`. Early reject, `q` untouched. |\n79\t| 7.15 | same | `p` = `01 00 … 00` (Edwards identity) → `ge25519_has_small_order(&P) != 0` | `-1`. Early reject, `q` untouched. |\n80\t| 7.16 | same | `p` is any of the other 7 torsion encodings (`00 00 … 00` order 4; `ec ff … 7f` order 2; the two order-4 and four order-8 points) → `ge25519_has_small_order` | `-1`. Note `ge25519_has_small_order` tests `X==0 ∨ Y==0 ∨ Z==0 ∨ Y·√-1 == X ∨ Y·√-1 == -X` (`ed25519_ref10.c:1173-1189`). Early reject, `q` untouched. |\n81\t| 7.17 | same | `p` is a valid, canonical, non-small-order point that is **not** in the prime-order subgroup 